course: world
lesson: 50
title: Thinking like a geographer: rivers, mountains and deserts
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Rivers flow to the sea because gravity pulls water downhill, and the sea is the lowest place it can reach; the sea does not overflow, because the Sun lifts just as much water from it by evaporation as the rivers bring.
explain: The sea is salty because rivers carry tiny amounts of salt washed from rocks; when sea water evaporates, only the water rises and the salt stays behind, so over millions of years the salt has built up.
explain: High in the mountains it is colder, even though you are a little closer to the Sun: sunlight warms the ground, the ground warms the air, and air that rises expands in the thinner air above and cools, by about 6 degrees for every kilometre up; so snow lies on Kilimanjaro in Africa, near the equator.
explain: Rain falls when moist air rises and cools; where air cannot rise, rain does not fall, even beside an ocean: along the coasts of Chile and Namibia a cold sea current chills the air from below, the cool air stays low, and the Atacama and Namib deserts lie right next to the water.
explain: Mountains make deserts too: wet wind from the sea climbs the slope, cools and drops its rain on that side, and arrives dry on the far side, which lies in a rain shadow.
fact: Water flows downhill because gravity pulls it down, and the sea is lower than the land around it.
fact: The sea does not overflow because as much water evaporates from it as rivers pour in.
fact: When salt water evaporates, the water leaves and the salt stays behind.
fact: The air gets about 6 degrees colder for every 1,000 metres you climb.
fact: The air is warmed mostly by the ground, which is warmed by sunlight.
fact: Rising air expands and cools, because the air higher up is thinner and presses less.
fact: Rain forms when moist air rises and cools so that its water vapour turns into droplets.
fact: Cold sea currents along some coasts cool the air from below and keep it from rising, so little rain falls there.
fact: The Atacama Desert in Chile, beside the Pacific Ocean, is one of the driest places on Earth.
fact: A rain shadow is the dry side of a mountain range, where the wind arrives after dropping its rain.
fact: Water warms up and cools down more slowly than land, so places by the sea have milder winters and cooler summers.
fact: A desert is a place where very little rain or snow falls, so Antarctica is also a desert.
exercise: Why do rivers flow towards the sea? | Water runs downhill, and the sea is lowest | The sea sucks the water in with its big waves | The wind always blows the rivers towards the sea | The Moon pulls all of the rivers towards the sea
exercise: Why does the sea not overflow, though rivers pour into it day and night? | As much water evaporates from it as the rivers bring | The bottom of the sea lets the water drain away | The sea is so big that it never fills up | Fish drink most of the water that arrives
exercise: Why is sea water salty while river water is almost fresh? | Evaporation takes away water but leaves salt behind | Fish make the salt in the sea | Salt falls into the sea with the rain and the snow each year | The sea floor is made of pure salt
exercise: Why is it colder on a mountain top than in the valley below? | Rising air spreads out and cools | The mountain top is farther from the Sun | Snow on the top makes the air cold | Wind brings cold air down from space
exercise: What mainly warms the air near the ground on a sunny day? | The ground, heated by sunlight | Sunlight passing straight through the air | Heat rising from the centre of the Earth | Warmth coming from the clouds
exercise: About how much colder is it 2,000 metres higher up a mountain? | About 12 degrees | About 2 degrees | About 50 degrees | The same temperature
exercise: How can there be snow on Kilimanjaro, so close to the equator? | Its top is high, where the air is freezing | Snow is carried there by winds from the poles | The equator is cold all year round, day and night | Kilimanjaro is in the shadow of the Moon all day
exercise: What has to happen to moist air for rain to fall? | It must rise and cool | It must sink and warm | It must stay still and warm | It must blow over hot sand
exercise: Why does the Atacama Desert lie right beside the Pacific Ocean? | A cold current keeps the air from rising to make rain | The ocean there is made of fresh water | Sand from the desert blocks the clouds | The Sun is closer to Chile than to any other place on Earth
exercise: What is a rain shadow? | The dry side of mountains where the wind arrives without rain | A cloud that hides the Sun before a storm | The dark patch under a tree in the rain | A place where it rains only at night
exercise: Why do towns by the sea have milder winters than towns far inland? | Water cools down slowly and keeps the air warmer | Sea water is heated from below by volcanoes all winter | Snow cannot fall near salt water | The Sun shines longer near the sea
exercise: Why is Antarctica counted as a desert? | Very little snow or rain falls there | It is covered with hot sand | It has no plants because it is too hot | Its ice is full of salt
exercise: Can a river flow from south to north? | Yes, if the land slopes north | No, rivers always flow from north to south | No, rivers always flow towards the equator | Only if the wind blows from the south
exercise: When sea water is left in a dish in the Sun for many days, what remains? | A crust of salt | Clean fresh water | Nothing at all | Sand from the sea floor
test: Lake A has rivers flowing in but none flowing out, and it is in a hot dry land. Lake B has a river flowing out to the sea. Which lake is more likely to be salty? | Lake A, because water only leaves it by evaporating | Lake B, because it is joined to the salty sea | Both are equally fresh | Both are equally salty
test: A climber starts at 1,000 metres where it is 20 degrees, and climbs to 3,000 metres. About what temperature should she expect? | About 8 degrees | About 26 degrees | About 18 degrees | About 0 degrees exactly
test: A hot-air balloon rises and the passengers are getting cold, though they are getting a little closer to the Sun. Why? | The air higher up is colder because it is thinner and farther from the warm ground | The balloon blocks the sunlight from reaching them | Being closer to the Sun always makes things colder | The fire of the balloon cools the air around it
test: A wet wind blows from the sea over a high mountain range. Where will the green forests most likely be? | On the slope facing the sea | On the far, dry side of the range | Only on the very top of the mountains | Equally on both sides
test: Which is true? | A place can be a desert even if it lies right beside an ocean | A desert must always be hot | Deserts are found only far from the sea | Wherever there is water nearby, rain falls often
test: Seattle is on the Pacific coast, and Minneapolis, about as far north, lies far inland. Which is most likely true? | Seattle has a milder winter than Minneapolis | Seattle has a colder winter than Minneapolis | Both always have the same weather | Seattle never has winter at all
test: If the Sun stopped evaporating water from the oceans, what would happen to the rivers after some time? | They would dry up, because no new rain would fall | They would flow faster than ever before, full of sea water | They would start flowing uphill | Nothing would change for the rivers
test: Why does the Mississippi flow south to the Gulf of Mexico and not north? | The land slopes down towards the Gulf of Mexico | Rivers in America must flow south by law of nature | The Gulf pulls water towards it with its salt | The north wind pushes the water south
test: Which shows that being nearer the Sun is NOT what makes a place warm? | Mountain tops are nearer the Sun but colder than valleys | Deserts are hot in the daytime | Summer is warmer than winter | The equator is hotter than the poles
test: if mountains are closer to the sun why's there snow on top | Rising air thins and cools, so it is colder higher up | The Sun cannot reach the peaks through the thick clouds | Snow is blown up from the sea | The rocks up there make cold
test: rivers keep pouring into the sea, why doesn't it overflow | It loses as much by evaporation | The sea leaks away into the Earth | The fish drink up all the extra water | The rivers all stop flowing in winter
test: my sister says Antarctica can't be a desert cos it's all ice. true? | No, a desert is a place with very little rain or snow, so it is one | Yes, deserts must be hot and sandy | Yes, ice makes it the wettest place | No, it is a desert only in summer
test: Liam reckons the sea is salty cos fish make the salt. right? | No, water evaporates and the salt carried in stays behind | Yes, fish give out salt | Yes, sailors poured salt in | No, the sea is salty because it is cold
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the sea does not overflow. | As much water evaporates from it as rivers pour in | The sea floor absorbs all river water like a great sponge | The rivers flow away from the sea | The Moon draws the excess into space
exam: State the approximate fall in air temperature for every thousand metres of ascent. | Approximately six degrees | Approximately sixty degrees | Approximately one degree | It does not fall at all
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term rain shadow? | The dry side of a mountain range | The shade cast by a raincloud | The wet side of a mountain range | A region of perpetual cloud
exam: Identify the principal reason for which coastal places have milder winters. | Water cools more slowly than land | The sea is heated from beneath | Coastal winds come from the equator | Coastal places are lower in altitude
